top for 2.11BSD UNIX / PDP-11
===========================

Build on the PDP:
    cd /home/dave/source/repos/pdpsrc/bsd/top
    make

Run from a Telnet terminal (root, or equivalent read access to the memory
and swap devices):
    TERM=vt100; export TERM
    ./top

The program uses the native 2.11BSD curses library and termcap, with
separate instruction and data spaces (-i). It does not use ncurses, /proc,
Linux interfaces, or subprocesses to collect its statistics.

Display
-------
The layout follows the supplied mentec reference: hostname/time/uptime,
user count and load averages; tasks; four CPU states; memory; swap; a blank
line; a reverse-video heading; then processes ordered by recent CPU use.

    PID USER      PR  NI  TEXT  DATA STACK S     TIME   DTIME COMMAND

TIME is cumulative user + system CPU time in seconds, excluding children.
DTIME is CPU seconds since the preceding sample. The first display follows
one sampling interval. Newly seen processes start with zero DTIME until
they have a preceding sample; PID plus start time distinguishes reuse.
Memory columns are KiB (1024 bytes); process segment sizes use the kernel's
64-byte clicks. Swap allocation uses 512-byte blocks. Used memory includes
kernel and other allocations, so it is not a sum of process sizes.

States: R running/runnable, S sleeping/waiting, T stopped, Z zombie.
A following 's' means the process is swapped out. '?' in the time fields
means that its user area was unavailable or changed during the sample.
Processes can exit or move while top reads memory; they do not abort the
monitor. Zombie exit accounting is read without interpreting its overlaid
live-process fields.

A wide terminal (at least 100 columns) shows the long reference summary.
The first line becomes compact when needed. Long commands are clipped
at the right margin rather than wrapping. Rows adapt to the terminal;
-n can limit the number of processes. Large terminals are capped at 160
columns, 50 rows and 5000 cells to fit the PDP-11's 64 KiB data space.
SIGWINCH resizes the curses screen. Terminal settings are restored on quit,
Ctrl-C, termination, and handled runtime errors.

The mentec telnetd accepts NAWS (Negotiate About Window Size) but does not
ask for it automatically. With the Mac's telnet client, press Ctrl-] and
enter this at the local telnet> prompt once per connection:

    send will naws

Return to the PDP session; resizing the Mac terminal will now update the
PDP's terminal size. Without NAWS, set the size manually before running:

    stty rows 30 columns 120

Use `stty size` to inspect the size that the PDP actually sees.

Options and keys
----------------
    -s seconds    Refresh interval, 0.1 to 1800 seconds (default 1).
    -o column     Sort column, 0..10 (default 9, DTIME then TIME).
    -n rows       Maximum processes shown (0 fills the screen).
    -I            Initially hide idle processes.
    -h            Print command-line help.

    q             Quit.
    Space         Refresh now.
    Ctrl-L        Redraw.
    I or i        Toggle idle processes.
    P             Sort by DTIME, then TIME.
    T             Sort by cumulative TIME.
    N             Sort by PID.
    M             Sort by data size.
    s             Prompt for refresh interval (Escape cancels).
    o             Prompt for sort column (Escape cancels).
    h or ?        Toggle interactive help.

Sort columns: 0 PID, 1 numeric UID, 2 priority, 3 nice, 4 text size,
5 data size, 6 stack size, 7 state, 8 TIME, 9 DTIME, 10 command.

Implementation notes
--------------------
This implementation was developed against the running mentec 2.11BSD
patch-level 498 kernel and its installed headers. /unix must match the
running kernel. It reads _proc, _nproc, _hz and _cp_time using nlist and
/dev/kmem. User structures come from /dev/mem or /dev/swap. Kernel sysctl
provides boot time, physical memory and allocation maps; getloadavg and
utmp provide load and login information. In this 2.11BSD kernel, sysctl
returns a byte count on success rather than zero.

The kernel interfaces and presentation were checked against the installed
2.11BSD ps, vmstat and top sources. The existing top was written by Digby
Tarvin, with later contributions including Johnny Billquist and other
2.11BSD maintainers. The system copy of top is not replaced by this build.
